92
00:07:01,467 --> 00:07:04,111
How does NetworkPolicies work?

93
00:07:04,667 --> 00:07:07,970
A NetworkPolicy is a firewall rule for Pods:

94
00:07:07,970 --> 00:07:13,301
"the database accepts connections only from the backend, on port 5432".

95
00:07:13,301 --> 00:07:20,809
It selects Pods by label and lists who may talk to them (ingress) or whom they may talk to (egress).

96
00:07:20,809 --> 00:07:21,710
The analogy:

97
00:07:21,710 --> 00:07:26,064
by default every office in the building has its door open.

98
00:07:26,064 --> 00:07:28,617
NetworkPolicies are badge readers:

99
00:07:28,617 --> 00:07:32,671
once a door has one, only the badges listed may enter.

100
00:07:33,233 --> 00:07:37,001
Lesson 21's model says every Pod can reach every Pod.

101
00:07:37,001 --> 00:07:39,346
That is convenient and dangerous:

102
00:07:39,346 --> 00:07:44,606
if an attacker takes over the frontend (the part exposed to the Internet),

103
00:07:44,606 --> 00:07:47,592
they can connect straight to the database.

104
00:07:47,592 --> 00:07:51,146
NetworkPolicies enforce the architecture you drew:

105
00:07:51,146 --> 00:07:54,558
frontend → backend → database, and nothing else.

106
00:07:56,300 --> 00:07:58,720
And how does it actually work?

107
00:07:59,267 --> 00:08:04,525
A Pod is isolated for ingress as soon as any policy with policyTypes:

108
